Negative control — `mapClientRecord` drops the custom-field values.

Mutates the `customFieldValues` member to a bare `[]` in place of
`raw.custom_fields ?? []`. The field is still present, still a `NonNullable`
array, still honours the `ClientRecord` contract for the absent case — so the
"defaults customFieldValues to a non-null array" guard stays GREEN. What breaks
is the verbatim carry: the recorded record's three custom-field values, nested
`field` relation intact, never reach the mapped record. Every sibling that
reads this one record then reads a stripped custom-field bag.

Declared subject — "maps the custom-field values verbatim, nested field
relation intact": the read-back asserts `customFieldValues` equals the
recorded `custom_fields` (length 3). A bare `[]` fails that equality, which is
exactly the drop this control exercises. Asserting only non-nullness would not
discriminate the drop from the real mapping; the verbatim equality does.

Single-subject: the other five cases read identity, profile, the
invoice-consolidation slice, and the absent-custom-fields default — none of
which this one-token swap touches. One RED, five GREEN.

